# SMPL station - mount an inserted SD card so the agent can read it.
#
# Install as:  /etc/udev/rules.d/99-smpl-sd-automount.rules
# Apply with:  sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
#
# Why this file exists at all
# ---------------------------
# Raspberry Pi OS *with a desktop* auto-mounts removable media through
# udisks2, and on such a machine this rule is unnecessary.  A headless Lite
# image mounts nothing: the card enumerates, /dev/sdb1 appears, and no
# filesystem is ever mounted, so the agent's watcher correctly reports that
# there is no card.  This is the piece that closes that gap.
#
# It replaces the old `usbmount` package, which is unmaintained and does not
# work on a systemd machine.  systemd-mount is the supported mechanism.
#
# Note the card being imported goes into a **USB card reader**.  The Pi's own
# microSD slot holds the operating system and is mounted at / - it is not, and
# must not become, an import source.
#
# Scope
# -----
# ENV{ID_BUS}=="usb" keeps this to removable readers and sticks.
# ENV{ID_FS_USAGE}=="filesystem" skips partition tables, swap and empty slots.
# ENV{UDISKS_IGNORE}!="1" leaves anything the desktop stack claimed alone, so
#   installing this on a desktop image does not produce two competing mounts.

ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{ID_BUS}=="usb", ENV{ID_FS_USAGE}=="filesystem", \
  ENV{UDISKS_IGNORE}!="1", \
  RUN{program}+="/usr/local/sbin/smpl-sd-mount.sh $devnode"

# On removal systemd-mount --collect tears its own unit down, so there is no
# unmount rule here on purpose: a second mechanism racing the first is how you
# get a stuck mount point that needs a reboot to clear.
